From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Richard Spindler <richard@lateralblast.com.au>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 19:30:00 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] channels: do not zero the extended data buffer on every pass

channel_handle_efd_read() declared its 32 KiB read buffer at the top of the
function and returned early, before using it, whenever the extended data fd
(a session's stderr) was not ready, which is nearly every pass of the event
loop. The build's -ftrivial-auto-var-init=zero zeroes an automatic array where
it is declared, so each call wrote 32 KiB of zeros for nothing.

Declare the buffer after the early return.

A perf profile of hpnsshd-session on Linux x86-64 during a loopback transfer
put 5.2 percent of the process's samples in that memset, under
channel_handle_efd_read(), and the total memset share fell from 8.5 to 4.8
percent with this change. Throughput did not change in the loopback test,
which the sending side limits, so this saves server CPU rather than speed.
---
 channels.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/channels.c b/channels.c
index 4000fa803..f19390cec 100644
--- a/channels.c
+++ b/channels.c
@@ -2543,7 +2543,6 @@ channel_handle_efd_write(struct ssh *ssh, Channel *c)
 static int
 channel_handle_efd_read(struct ssh *ssh, Channel *c)
 {
-	char buf[CHAN_RBUF];
 	ssize_t len;
 	int r, force;
 
@@ -2552,6 +2551,14 @@ channel_handle_efd_read(struct ssh *ssh, Channel *c)
 	if (!force && (c->io_ready & SSH_CHAN_IO_EFD_R) == 0)
 		return 1;
 
+	/*
+	 * Declared after the early return above, which is the common case:
+	 * this runs once per event loop pass for every session, and with
+	 * -ftrivial-auto-var-init=zero a buffer declared at the top is zeroed
+	 * (32 KiB) on every call, even when nothing is read.
+	 */
+	char buf[CHAN_RBUF];
+
 	len = read(c->efd, buf, sizeof(buf));
 	debug2("channel %d: read %zd from efd %d", c->self, len, c->efd);
 	if (len == -1 && (errno == EINTR || ((errno == EAGAIN ||
